   "note": "The original's effect-path JFET switch (between R9 and the follower base; type not given on either source) is ON in the effect state and is a closed path here, as on the Tonepad schematic; its few-hundred-ohm channel is in series with a 1 k into a base of several Mohm. The dry-path JFET from the IC1a buffer ('A') is OFF and omitted. Batch-7 re-read (2026-11-12, Tech-21-SansAmp-GT-2.pdf at 300 dpi, crop 3050,100,3450,450): the two devices between R9/'A' and Q1's base are N-channel JFETs (d/s/g lettered, channel bar with the d and s leads, gate arrow pointing in), each with a series diode on its gate (anode at the gate) to an undrawn stub - the classic JFET bypass switch driven by the footswitch logic. 'A' is the IC1a input buffer's output, i.e. the dry signal: the pair selects dry or effect into the Q1 follower, matching the manual's 'custom footswitch actuator ... buffered bypass' (GT2-OM.pdf, Product Overview). They are NOT clippers; the GT2 clips at its op-amp rails, as this description says. A scratch build with both JFETs (2N5457 card, effect gate floating via 10M, dry gate held by its diode at 0 V) measured within 0.03 dB and 0.01 % THD of this closed path at Drive 0/0.5/1, and the dry leak at Level 0 unchanged (-54.2 dB at 1 kHz), so the switch stays modelled closed: its type and gate drive are not on either source."
